@@ -44,6 +44,30 @@ Pi's extensions and packages.
 | Pi extensions | Each Pi here, this computer's and each WSL distro's (`piPlaces`), with the extensions and packages it loads, read by Pi's own rules (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` 1.4: `resolveExtensionEntries`, `isEnabledByOverrides`, `applyPatterns`). An extension is a `.ts`/`.js` file or a folder (package.json `pi.extensions`, else `index.ts`, else `index.js`) in `<agentDir>/extensions`, or a path its settings.json `extensions` lists; a package is an entry of settings.json `packages`. Built-in extensions (`builtin:mcp` and the rest) aren't listed: `builtin:mcp` carries the MCP servers magpie gives Pi. `SetPiExtension` writes what `pi config` writes (`-path` off; the `-` taken out and `+path` only where still excluded, on); `SetPiPackage` sets a package's four filters to `[]` (off) or takes them out (on), and refuses an entry that already picks some parts (`filtered`, changed in `pi config`). `CopyPi` gives a package to another Pi as its settings.json entry, which Pi installs itself when it next starts (npm into `<agentDir>/npm`, git into `<agentDir>/git`), never as copied files; it refuses a local package, and one the other Pi has already by Pi's identity (npm name, git host/path). An extension is copied as its file or folder into the other's `extensions`, leaving out `node_modules`, `.git` and symlinks, never over one of the same name, and the page says to run `npm install` there when it has dependencies (leokon3 on Discord). A settings.json that can't be parsed lists nothing and is never written; a stopped distro is never started (`asleep`); every write is backed up first. `GET`/`POST /api/library/pi`, `POST /api/library/pi/copy`; the Library's Pi packages tab, shown while a Pi is here (`renderPi` in plugins/library/client.js; library-pi-extensions.test.cjs). | [`piext.go`](../../internal/library/piext.go) |
 | Pi market | `MarketPiPackages` lists npm's packages with the keyword `pi-package`, the way pi.dev's gallery finds them: the 500 most downloaded (two pages of npm's search, sorted by last month's downloads), cached 6h in memory and on disk, else the bundled `market_pi.json`; a search of two or more characters adds npm's own `keywords:pi-package <q>` search, and a search npm doesn't answer keeps the popular matches with a `SearchError` (the page says so). Every name is held to npm's rules (`piNameRe`), and a repository link is https only. `InstallPiPackage` runs `pi install npm:<name>` with `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` set to that Pi's folder (`piRun`), then checks settings.json lists it; `RemovePiPackage` runs `pi remove <source>`, which drops every entry of the same identity and uninstalls it. A Pi with no `pi` here (a WSL distro's, or this computer's with no CLI on PATH) gets the settings.json entry written, or taken out, instead, and Pi installs it when it next starts (`pending`). Both refuse an unreadable settings.json, install refuses a package the Pi has by Pi's identity, and each write is backed up first. `GET /api/library/market/pi`, `POST /api/library/pi/install`, `POST /api/library/pi/remove`; Discover under the Pi packages tab (`piCard`, `installPi`, `confirmRemovePi` in plugins/library/client.js; library-pi-market.test.cjs) marks a package every running Pi has Installed, and Install asks which Pi when several lack it (nineruiing on X). | [`pimarket.go`](../../internal/library/pimarket.go) |
 
+## Skills published by a website
+
+`ProbeSkills` also accepts an HTTP(S) website address, including one pasted
+as `npx skills add <address>`. A website can publish the skills CLI's
+well-known file-list index: `skills` entries with `name`, `description` and
+`files`, served under `/.well-known/skills/`. This is a separate source
+(`kind: well-known`), not a git repository. See
+[`skillwellknown.go`](../../internal/library/skillwellknown.go).
+
+The website address is kept as the source; discovery never visits its home
+page to replace it with a redirect's destination. A scoped address stays in
+that scope. Every listed supporting file is installed with `SKILL.md`, after
+the manifest's paths and limits have been checked. No downloaded content is
+executed, and no GitHub token or agent credential is sent to the website.
+
+These skills are grouped by their website, with a source link and the same
+update controls as repository skills. Checks compare the fetched content
+with the installed hash. Updates retain the existing protection for local
+edits and the existing backup and replacement path. An unreadable or invalid
+index, an incomplete download or an invalid skill is an error, not evidence
+that the skill was removed. A skill is marked removed only after its source
+was read successfully without it. Archive-based discovery manifests are not
+supported by this file-list reader.
+
 ## Runtime path
 
 1. **Edit.** The Library page or `magpie library` calls an exported function such as `SaveServer`, `ServerAgents`, `InstallSkills`, `SkillAgents` or `SetSkillHow`. The skills and servers pages each have Select, Turn all on, Turn all off and Remove all; the heading shows Select (and a busy Checking…, Grouping… or Updating…) and keeps the rest under its ⋯, the app's row menu (`barMore` in plugins/library/client.js). Select's bar calls `SomeSkillsAgents` or `SomeServersAgents` (`/api/library/{skills,servers}/agents-some`), Turn all on or off calls `EverySkillAgents` or `EveryServerAgents` for the agents shown, and Remove all calls `RemoveSkills` or `RemoveServers`. The hooks page has Select and Remove all too (`SomeHooksAgents`, `RemoveHooks`). Each of these is one change that takes every item out as a single removal would (#1027). Select's bar also has Remove… (#1852), which asks first, naming the count, and sends only the picked names to `RemoveSkills`, `RemoveServers` or `RemoveHooks`; ticking a group's or part's header picks all of it. A name that wasn't removed stays picked, with its error in its row's place (`pickErrs`), and the status names it; when every name fails the endpoint answers 400 and the sheet stays open. A row's agent chips start with All (`allChip` in `plugins/library/client.js`), which gives the item to every agent shown that can take it, or takes it from them all; the pickers that choose the agents before an item is added (installing skills from a repository or the market, adding, bringing in or installing a server) have the same All: most start with every agent lit, and one agent alone took a click on each of the others (#1531).
